data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 10, the EF Core Cosmos DB provider deprecated synchronous LINQ execution but still allowed it via the sync-over-async opt-in (`AllowSynchronousIO`). **In .NET 11, that opt-in is removed entirely.** Calling any synchronous LINQ operator (`ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, etc.) on the Cosmos DB provider now throws an `InvalidOperationException` at runtime.

### Every method in your repository breaks:

| Call | Problem |
|---|---|
| `.ToList()` | ❌ Throws |
| `.First(...)` | ❌ Throws |
| `.Count()` | ❌ Throws |
| `.SaveChanges()` | ❌ Throws |

### The fix — convert to async:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key changes:**
- Return `Task<T>` / `Task` and mark methods `async`
- `ToList()` → `ToListAsync()`, `First()` → `FirstAsync()`, `Count()` → `CountAsync()`, `SaveChanges()` → `SaveChangesAsync()`
- Rename methods with `Async` suffix (convention)
- All callers up the chain must also be made async (`async`/`await` propagates)

> **Note:** `Add()` stays synchronous — it only tracks the entity in memory. The actual I/O happens in `SaveChangesAsync()`.
